A **mode switch** (user ↔ kernel) is just a trap or return. The CPU changes its privilege bit, switches to the kernel stack and jumps to a handler. The same process keeps running in the same address space.

A **process switch** (context switch) needs much more:
1. a switch into kernel mode (the scheduler is part of the kernel), so it already contains at least one mode switch;
2. saving the complete CPU context of the old process into its PCB;
3. running the scheduler to pick the next process;
4. loading the new process's context and switching the address space (page-table base register, often a TLB flush, with cache and TLB misses afterwards);
5. a switch back to user mode in the new process.

A process switch therefore includes mode switches plus a lot of extra work, so $t_1 < t_2$.

**Answer: (C)**

