Consider the following scheme for implementing a critical section in a situation with three processes $P_i, P_j$ and $P_k$.

Pi;

```pascal
repeat
  flag[i] := true;
  while flag [j] or flag[k] do
    case turn of
    j: if flag [j] then
    begin
      flag [i] := false;
      while turn != i do skip;
      flag [i] := true;
    end;
    k: if flag [k] then
    begin
      flag [i] := false,
      while turn != i do skip;
      flag [i] := true
    end
  end
  critical section
  if turn = i then turn := j;
    flag [i] := false
  non-critical section
until false;
```

Is there a situation in which a waiting process can never enter the critical section? If so, explain and suggest modifications to the code to solve this problem

go_v3_5.24.6.e:
**Yes.** The trouble is the variable `turn`. It is changed only by the process that holds it, when that process leaves the CS (`if turn = i then turn := j`, so the turn moves $i \to j \to k \to i$), and a process that has backed off waits until `turn` equals its own id. If `turn` rests with a process that is not interested in the CS, nobody ever moves it.

*Situation 1 — deadlock.* Let `turn = i` while $P_i$ stays in its non-critical section. $P_j$ and $P_k$ both set their flags and enter the `while` loop. For each of them the case branch for `turn = i` tests `flag[i]`, which is false, so neither backs off; and each keeps looping because the other one's flag is true. Neither ever enters the CS.

*Situation 2 — starvation.* $P_i$ holds the turn and is in the CS. $P_k$ arrives, finds `flag[i]` true with `turn = i`, lowers `flag[k]` and waits for `turn = k`. $P_i$ exits and sets `turn := j`. If $P_j$ never requests the CS again, `turn` stays $j$ for ever, so $P_k$ waits for ever — while $P_i$ can keep re-entering, because it sees `flag[k] = false`.

**Modification.** Keep a separate indicator `want[x]` that stays true while $P_x$ is requesting (even after it has backed off), let a contender take the turn when the holder is not requesting, and on exit hand the turn to the next *requesting* process in cyclic order:

```pascal
repeat
  want[i] := true;
  flag[i] := true;
  while flag[j] or flag[k] do
  begin
    if not want[turn] then turn := i;     { holder not interested: take the turn }
    if turn <> i then                     { a requesting process holds the turn }
    begin
      flag[i] := false;                   { back off }
      while (turn <> i) and want[turn] do skip;
      flag[i] := true
    end
  end;
  critical section
  if turn = i then                        { pass the turn on, cyclically }
    if want[j] then turn := j
    else if want[k] then turn := k;
  flag[i] := false;
  want[i] := false;
  non-critical section
until false;
```

- Mutual exclusion is unaffected: a process still enters only after raising its own flag and seeing both other flags false.
- No deadlock: `turn` always settles on a requesting process; that process never backs off while every other contender does, so it gets in.
- No starvation: the holder passes the turn to the next requester in cyclic order, so a waiting process obtains the turn after a bounded number of entries by the others.

(Equivalently, one could replace the scheme by a known correct $n$-process algorithm such as Eisenberg–McGuire or Lamport's bakery algorithm.)

**Answer: Yes — when `turn` belongs to a process that does not want the CS, the competing processes can loop for ever (or a backed-off process can wait for ever); fix it by letting contenders take an unused turn and passing the turn only to requesting processes.**

go_v3_5.24.7.e:
**Idea:** run every statement as a separate concurrent process inside `parbegin … parend` and give each edge $X \to Y$ its own semaphore, initialised to 0: $X$ does a `V` on it after finishing, $Y$ does a `P` on it before starting. A statement therefore starts only after all of its predecessors have completed.

Semaphores: $a: S_1 \to S_2$, $b: S_1 \to S_3$, $c: S_2 \to S_4$, $d: S_2 \to S_5$, $e: S_3 \to S_5$, $f: S_6 \to S_4$, $g: S_6 \to S_5$.

```pascal
var a, b, c, d, e, f, g : semaphore;   { all initialised to 0 }
begin
  parbegin
    begin S1; V(a); V(b) end;
    begin P(a); S2; V(c); V(d) end;
    begin P(b); S3; V(e) end;
    begin P(c); P(f); S4 end;
    begin P(d); P(e); P(g); S5 end;
    begin S6; V(f); V(g) end
  parend
end.
```

$S_1$ and $S_6$ have no predecessors and start at once; $S_2$ and $S_3$ wait for $S_1$; $S_4$ waits for $S_2$ and $S_6$; $S_5$ waits for $S_2$, $S_3$ and $S_6$.

Fewer semaphores also work, e.g. one counting semaphore per statement: $S_4$ does `P(x4); P(x4)` while $S_2$ and $S_6$ each do `V(x4)`; $S_5$ does `P(x5)` three times while $S_2$, $S_3$, $S_6$ each do `V(x5)`.

Semaphores are really needed here: the graph is not series–parallel ($S_2$ precedes both $S_4$ and $S_5$, while $S_3$ precedes only $S_5$), so nested parbegin–parend blocks alone could not express it without losing parallelism.

**Answer: One semaphore per edge inside parbegin–parend, as in the program above.**

