A concurrent system consists of $3$ processes using a shared resource $R$ in a non-preemptible and mutually exclusive manner. The processes have unique priorities in the range $1 \ldots 3$, $3$ being the highest priority. It is required to synchronize the processes such that the resource is always allocated to the highest priority requester. The pseudo code for the system is as follows.

**Shared data**

```pascal
mutex:semaphore = 1:/* initialized to 1*/

process[3]:semaphore = 0; /*all initialized to 0 */

R_requested [3]:boolean = false; /*all initialized to false */

busy: boolean = false; /*initialized to false */
```

**Code for processes**

```pascal
begin process
my-priority:integer;
my-priority:=___; /*in the range 1..3*/
repeat
    request_R(my-priority);
    P (proceed [my-priority]);
    {use shared resource R}
    release_R (my-priority);
forever
end process;
```

**Procedures**

```pascal
procedure request_R(priority);
P(mutex);
if busy = true then
    R_requested [priority]:=true;
else
begin
    V(proceed [priority]);
    busy:=true;
end
V(mutex)
```

Give the pseudo code for the procedure release_R.

go_v3_5.24.14.e:
The semaphore array declared as `process[3]` is the one used as `proceed[1..3]`: `proceed[p]` is the private semaphore on which the process of priority $p$ waits. `busy` records whether $R$ is allocated; a requester that finds $R$ busy records itself in `R_requested[p]` and then blocks on `proceed[p]`.

When the holder releases $R$ it must, inside `mutex`, **hand $R$ directly to the highest-priority pending requester**, leaving `busy = true` so that nobody else can grab $R$ in between; only if nobody is waiting does it mark $R$ free.

```pascal
procedure release_R(priority);
var p : integer;
begin
  P(mutex);
  p := 3;
  while (p >= 1) and not R_requested[p] do   { find highest-priority waiter }
    p := p - 1;
  if p >= 1 then
  begin
    R_requested[p] := false;    { its request is now being served }
    V(proceed[p])               { wake it up; R stays busy }
  end
  else
    busy := false;              { no requester: R becomes free }
  V(mutex)
end;
```

Why this is correct:
- `busy` and `R_requested[ ]` are read and updated only under `mutex`, so a concurrent `request_R` cannot interleave with the hand-over.
- `busy` stays true during a hand-over, so a newly arriving process is recorded as a requester instead of being given $R$ ahead of a higher-priority waiter.
- Scanning from priority 3 downwards gives $R$ to the highest-priority requester, as required.
- Each priority has its own semaphore, so `V(proceed[p])` wakes exactly that process (even if it has not yet reached its `P`, the semaphore remembers the signal). The releaser's own `priority` parameter is not needed.

**Answer: Inside `P(mutex) … V(mutex)`: pick the highest $p$ with `R_requested[p] = true`; if one exists, set `R_requested[p] := false` and `V(proceed[p])` (keep `busy = true`); otherwise `busy := false`.**

