   produce an item;
   p1:.................;
   put the item into buff (first);
   first = (first+1)%BUFFSIZE;
   p2: ...............;
   }
}

void consumer()
{
while(1) {
   c1:............
   take the item from buf[last];
   last = (last+1)%BUFFSIZE;
   c2:............;
   consume the item;
   }
}
```

A. Complete the dotted part of the above solution.

B. Using another semaphore variable, insert one line statement each immediately after $p1$, immediately before $p2$, immediately after $c1$ and immediately before $c2$ so that the program works correctly for multiple producers and consumers.

go_v3_5.24.23.e:
**Key idea.** `b_empty` counts free slots (initially `BUFFSIZE`) and `b_full` counts filled slots (initially 0). A producer must first claim a free slot and then announce a filled slot. The consumer does the reverse.

**A. Dotted parts**

- `p1: P(b_empty);` waits for a free slot (blocks when the buffer is full).
- `p2: V(b_full);` announces one more available item.
- `c1: P(b_full);` waits for an item (blocks when the buffer is empty).
- `c2: V(b_empty);` announces one more free slot.

With one producer and one consumer this is enough. The producer only touches `first` and the consumer only touches `last`, and the counting semaphores keep them from working on the same slot.

**B. Multiple producers and consumers.** Now two producers could read the same `first`, write into the same slot and update `first` incorrectly; consumers can collide on `last` in the same way. Add `semaphore mutex = 1;` and make the buffer and index update a critical section:

```c
/* producer */
p1: P(b_empty);
    P(mutex);                 /* inserted after p1  */
    put the item into buff(first);
    first = (first+1)%BUFFSIZE;
    V(mutex);                 /* inserted before p2 */
p2: V(b_full);

/* consumer */
c1: P(b_full);
    P(mutex);                 /* inserted after c1  */
    take the item from buf[last];
    last = (last+1)%BUFFSIZE;
    V(mutex);                 /* inserted before c2 */
c2: V(b_empty);
```

`P(mutex)` must come **after** the counting `P`. If a process took `mutex` first and then blocked on `b_empty` or `b_full`, no other process could get in to change that count, which would cause deadlock.

**Answer: A: P(b_empty), V(b_full), P(b_full), V(b_empty). B: P(mutex)/V(mutex) around the buffer access in both producer and consumer, with mutex = 1.**

